Sentence 1: Virtual acoustic space
Virtual acoustic space Virtual acoustic space (VAS), also known as virtual auditory space, is a technique in which sounds presented over headphones appear to originate from any desired direction in space.
Sentence 2: The illusion of a virtual sound source outside the listener's head is created.
Sentence 3: Sound localization cues generate an externalized percept When one listens to sounds over headphones (in what is known as the "closed field") the sound source appears to arise from center of the head.
Sentence 4: On the other hand, under normal, so-called free-field, listening conditions sounds are perceived as being externalized.
Sentence 5: The direction of a sound in space
Sentence 6: Sound localization
Sound localization Sound localization is a listener's ability to identify the location or origin of a detected sound in direction and distance.
Sentence 7: The sound localization mechanisms of the mammalian auditory system have been extensively studied.
Sentence 8: The auditory system uses several cues for sound source localization, including time- and level-differences (or intensity-difference) between both ears, spectral information, timing analysis, correlation analysis, and pattern matching.
