Sentence 1: Aesti
Aesti The Aesti (also Aestii, Astui or Aests) were an ancient people first described by the Roman historian Tacitus in his treatise "Germania" (circa 98 AD).
Sentence 2: According to Tacitus, the territory of Aesti was located somewhere east of the "Suiones" (Swedes) and west of the "Sitones" (possibly the ancient "Kvens"), on the Suebian (Baltic) Sea.
Sentence 3: The Aesti (Easterners), recorded by Tacitus, were 450 years later recorded by Jordanes as part of the Gothic Empire.
Sentence 4: The territory and inhabitants were described by Tacitus in Germania in 98, where Suebi, Goths and other Germanic people lived on both sides of the Vistula River, adjacent to the Aesti (further east).
Sentence 5: About 800 to 900 years later the Aesti were named Old Prussians, who, since 997, repeatedly defended themselves against take-over attempts by the newly created Duchy
